//? Title: ChaCha20: Actual ARX Stages, RFC Vectors and Counter Limits
//? Subtitle: Checked one-shot XOR, native state tracing and the limits of a byte histogram
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A stream cipher expands a key, nonce and counter into bytes that can be XORed
with a message without changing its length. Correct byte order, round scheduling
and counter limits matter even when encryption and decryption appear to round-trip.
We inspect a four-word quarter round, reproduce the complete RFC 8439 block and
ciphertext vectors, and retain the original 4 KiB histogram. Native C++ now records
actual ARX stages, block states and XOR block use, rejects complete-call counter
wrap, and supplies binary hex and histogram helpers. A constant-byte control
shows why the histogram's mean does not establish uniformity. Raw XOR also does
not authenticate a message. These are executed conformance and mechanism checks,
not a security proof or a machine-code timing audit.
)

\toc

> A four-word quarter round, small enough to inspect

Use the public RFC input a=11111111, b=01020304, c=9b8d6f43,
d=01234567 in hexadecimal. Each addition is modulo 2^32. The first
addition gives a=12131415; XOR and rotation give d=51721330.
The next group updates c and b. Two more groups produce the published
output ea2a92f4, cb1cf8ce, 4581472e, 5881c4bb. The table and
figure use intermediate values from the actual shared quarter-round kernel.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chacha20_quarter.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::chacha20_quarter_trace({0x11111111,0x01020304,0x9b8d6f43,0x01234567});
  const std::array<std::uint32_t,4> expected{0xea2a92f4,0xcb1cf8ce,0x4581472e,0x5881c4bb};
  if(trace.output!=expected) return 1;
  const std::array<int,4> rotations{16,12,8,7};
  std::print("| compound group | rotation | a | b | c | d |\n| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1050);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=4.8;node.height=2.8;node.font_size=18;
  const std::array<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint,4> positions{{{0,0},{8,0},{8,-4},{0,-4}}};
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<4;++i) {
    const auto& state=trace.stages[i];
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:08x} | {:08x} | {:08x} | {:08x} |\n",i+1,rotations[i],state[0],state[1],state[2],state[3]);
    if(i==3) node.fill={220,252,231};
    diagram.node(std::format("group{}",i),positions[i],
        std::format("group {}: rotate {}\na {:08x}   b {:08x}\nc {:08x}   d {:08x}",i+1,rotations[i],state[0],state[1],state[2],state[3]),node);
    if(i) diagram.edge(std::format("group{}",i-1),std::format("group{}",i),"next");
  }
  diagram.text({4,2.5},"RFC quarter-round input: 11111111, 01020304, 9b8d6f43, 01234567",18)
      .text({4,-6.3},"actual add / XOR / rotate states; all additions modulo 2^32",18)
      .save_svg("build/chacha20_quarter.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| compound group | rotation | a | b | c | d |
| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | 16 | 12131415 | 01020304 | 9b8d6f43 | 51721330 |
| 2 | 12 | 12131415 | d8177edf | ecff8273 | 51721330 |
| 3 | 8 | ea2a92f4 | d8177edf | ecff8273 | 5881c4bb |
| 4 | 7 | ea2a92f4 | cb1cf8ce | 4581472e | 5881c4bb |
\image(figures/chacha20_quarter.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Four actual compound ARX stages, using the quarter-round vector from RFC 8439 Section 2.1.1.)
\alttext(Four boxes follow rotations sixteen, twelve, eight and seven, showing each pair of changed words and the final published four-word output.)

The groups are a+=b, d=ROTL(d XOR a,16); c+=d,
b=ROTL(b XOR c,12); then the same pattern with rotations 8 and 7.
A rotation, XOR with a known word, and modular addition each have inverses.
Reversing the group order therefore recovers the quarter-round input; native
tests check that on 10,000 inputs. Encryption does not invert this permutation:
it generates the same keystream and cancels XOR instead.

> Columns, diagonals, feed-forward and little-endian bytes

The RFC block has sixteen 32-bit words: four constants, eight key words,
one counter and three nonce words. One round applies four column quarter
rounds; the next uses diagonals. Ten such pairs give 20 rounds and 80
quarter rounds. Adding the original words modulo 2^32 and serializing each
word least-significant byte first gives 64 keystream bytes. Feed-forward is
part of the specified construction; its presence alone is not a proof of
one-wayness or secrecy.

This program checks all 64 bytes of RFC 8439 Section 2.3.2, rather than
only the old report's first 32. Its state table comes from actual setup,
permutation and feed-forward records. The first diagonal selects indices
[0,5,10,15], whereas the first column selects [0,4,8,12].

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chacha20_block.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::array<std::uint8_t,32> key{};std::iota(key.begin(),key.end(),std::uint8_t{0});
  const std::array<std::uint8_t,12> nonce{0,0,0,9,0,0,0,0x4a,0,0,0,0};
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::chacha20_block_trace(key,1,nonce);
  const auto hex=dm::algorithms::to_hex(trace.output);
  const std::string expected="10f1e7e4d13b5915500fdd1fa32071c4c7d1f4c733c068030422aa9ac3d46c4e"
      "d2826446079faa0914c2d705d98b02a2b5129cd1de164eb9cbd083e8a2503c4e";
  if(hex!=expected || trace.quarter_rounds.size()!=80) return 1;
  std::print("| word index | initial | after 20 rounds | after feed-forward |\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<16;++i) std::print("| {} | {:08x} | {:08x} | {:08x} |\n",i,trace.initial[i],trace.round_states[19][i],trace.feed_forward[i]);
  std::print("\nFirst column indices: {}. First diagonal indices: {}.\n",trace.quarter_rounds[0].indices,trace.quarter_rounds[4].indices);
  std::print("Complete RFC block matches: {}.\n",hex==expected);
  for(std::size_t offset=0;offset<64;offset+=16) std::print("Bytes {:02}..{:02}: {}\n",offset,offset+15,hex.substr(2*offset,32));
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=5.1;node.height=2.2;
  diagram.node("initial",{0,0},std::format("16 initial words\nconstant + key + counter + nonce\nword 0 = {:08x}",trace.initial[0]),node);
  diagram.node("mixed",{8,0},std::format("20 actual round states\n{} quarter rounds\nword 0 = {:08x}",trace.quarter_rounds.size(),trace.round_states[19][0]),node);
  node.fill={220,252,231};
  diagram.node("sum",{8,-4},std::format("add original words modulo 2^32\nword 0 = {:08x}",trace.feed_forward[0]),node)
      .node("bytes",{0,-4},std::format("serialize little-endian\nfirst four bytes: {}",hex.substr(0,8)),node)
      .edge("initial","mixed","copy, mix").edge("mixed","sum","mixed state")
      .edge("initial","sum","feed-forward").edge("sum","bytes","serialize")
      .text({4,-6},"one actual block: 16-word setup, 80 quarter rounds, 64 output bytes",18)
      .save_svg("build/chacha20_block.svg");
}
```
