\caption(The original grouped histogram, regenerated from actual native output. The red line is the uniform-model expectation; the table's constant control has the same mean.)
\alttext(Sixteen blue bars show observed high-nibble counts among four thousand ninety-six keystream bytes, with a red reference at two hundred fifty-six. A table shows that a constant-zero sample has the same average.)

> Counter bounds, chunk boundaries and unauthenticated changes

A raw starting counter c has exactly 2^32-c available blocks, including
UINT32_MAX. The complete byte capacity is

$$
B(c)=64(2^{32}-c),\qquad N(n)=\lfloor n/64\rfloor+[n\bmod64\ne0].
$$
\alttext(Byte capacity is sixty-four times two to the thirty-two minus the initial counter. Required blocks equal the integer quotient by sixty-four plus one if a remainder exists.)

The whole message must fit that capacity. The new preflight uses division
rather than n+63, so even a maximal uint64 byte-count query is safe. It rejects
an overflowing range before copying or processing a message. Counter zero has
2^38 bytes of raw capacity; starting at one leaves one fewer block. At the
final counter, exactly 64 bytes are allowed and 65 are rejected. This local
range check does not track reuse across separate calls with the same key and nonce.

Each call starts at the first byte of its chosen counter. Splitting at byte
64 and advancing the counter preserves the original stream; splitting at byte
63 and advancing discards a byte and changes the result. A caller needing
arbitrary incremental reads needs a stateful stream abstraction. This report
keeps the existing one-shot API and makes its behavior explicit.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  constexpr auto last=std::numeric_limits<std::uint32_t>::max();
  const auto capacity=dm::algorithms::chacha20_capacity_bytes(0);
  if(dm::algorithms::chacha20_blocks_required(0,capacity)!=4294967296ULL) return 1;
  const auto final=dm::algorithms::chacha20_encrypt({},last,{},std::string(64,'x'));
  bool rejected=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::chacha20_encrypt({},last,{},std::string(65,'x'));}
  catch(const std::length_error&) {rejected=true;}
  bool cap=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::chacha20_encrypt_trace({},1,{},std::string(65,'x'),1);}
  catch(const std::length_error&) {cap=true;}
  const std::string plain(100,'L');const auto whole=dm::algorithms::chacha20_encrypt({},1,{},plain);
  const auto aligned=dm::algorithms::chacha20_encrypt({},1,{},plain.substr(0,64))+dm::algorithms::chacha20_encrypt({},2,{},plain.substr(64));
  const auto misaligned=dm::algorithms::chacha20_encrypt({},1,{},plain.substr(0,63))+dm::algorithms::chacha20_encrypt({},2,{},plain.substr(63));
  auto changed=whole;changed[0]^=1;const auto altered=dm::algorithms::chacha20_encrypt({},1,{},changed);
  if(!rejected || !cap || aligned!=whole || misaligned==whole || altered[0]!='M' || altered.substr(1)!=plain.substr(1)) return 1;
  std::print("Raw capacity at counter zero: {} bytes; at final counter: {} bytes.\n",capacity,dm::algorithms::chacha20_capacity_bytes(last));
  std::print("Final-counter output bytes: {}; 65-byte range rejected: {}; one-block trace cap rejected: {}.\n",final.size(),rejected,cap);
  std::print("Aligned 64-byte split matches: {}; misaligned 63-byte split matches: {}.\n",aligned==whole,misaligned==whole);
  std::print("Changed ciphertext bit decrypts first byte L to {}; remaining bytes unchanged: {}.\n",altered[0],altered.substr(1)==plain.substr(1));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Raw capacity at counter zero: 274877906944 bytes; at final counter: 64 bytes.
Final-counter output bytes: 64; 65-byte range rejected: true; one-block trace cap rejected: true.
Aligned 64-byte split matches: true; misaligned 63-byte split matches: false.
Changed ciphertext bit decrypts first byte L to M; remaining bytes unchanged: true.
// result_end

The shared core uses fixed unsigned-word ARX operations without secret-indexed
S-box tables. That source-level property is not a machine-code constant-time
audit. Native traces retain key or keystream material and do not erase memory;
all exposed values here use public test inputs. The implementation supplies no
nonce manager or authentication layer. These limits are separate from whether
its bytes conform to the RFC.

Ordinary XOR takes O(n) work and owned output storage, with constant block
scratch. Block traces retain 80 actual quarter-round records and 20 state
snapshots; encryption traces retain the actual counters, offsets, byte counts
and full keystream blocks, including unused tails. No round or encryption is
replayed to manufacture records. A positive trace block cap rejects the whole
call before block allocation/work. Binary strings and views preserve embedded
NULs and high-bit bytes; reusable hex and histogram helpers replace report-local
byte-processing code. No observational dataset is needed for exact cipher
conformance or these controlled binary counterexamples.

Native verification includes complete published quarter-round, block-state,
keystream and ciphertext values. An independent block oracle performs diagonal
rounds by rotating rows, processing columns and restoring rows. It checks 500
random blocks and all recorded steps; 500 binary-message cases check actual XOR
records and round trips. Ten thousand quarter-round inputs match a widened
reference and reverse correctly. Counter endpoints, allocation-free capacity
queries, empty input, exact block caps, chunk alignment, tampering, ownership,
all byte values, histogram groups and the original crypto regressions also pass.

> References

- Y. Nir and A. Langley (2018). [RFC 8439: ChaCha20 and Poly1305 for IETF Protocols](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8439.html). Sections 2.1–2.4 define the primitive and published conformance vectors; Section 2.8 describes the separate authenticated construction.
